<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yan'an</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yenan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yan'an is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the Loess Plateau. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> it was the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> from 1937 to 1947, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> consolidated his leadership and the party developed its policies. The area was an old frontier district on the northern edge of the Chinese heartland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yanan.jpg" alt="Yan'an" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H2v5o68z · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baota_Mountain_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yan'an is a prefecture-level city in the Shaanbei region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province, on the Loess Plateau. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> to the east across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> to the west across the Ziwu Ridge. The land is hilly, ranging from 388.8 to 1,809.8 metres above sea level, and the Yellow River, the Yan River and the Luo River flow through it. Yaodong, cave dwellings cut into the hillsides, are traditional homes of northern Shaanxi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was populated at least as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-dynasty">Xia dynasty</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> was established in 221 BCE it formed part of Shang Commandery, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> organised it as Yan'an Commandery with a military base. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> it was a border fortress against the Tangut-led Western Xia, and in 1080 the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> was sent to Yan Prefecture to aid its defence; in autumn 1081 he defended Song territory and captured several nearby fortified towns. In 1221 combined Mongol and Western Xia forces defeated a Jin army of 30,000 in the east of the present-day area.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-revolt">Dungan Revolt</a> of 1862-1877 much of rural Yan'an was inhabited by Chinese Muslims, many of whom were killed or forced to move; Bao'an had 51,000 people in 1823 but only 170 in 1868.</p><p>In October 1935, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-red-army">Chinese Red Army</a> arrived in the area from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>. In January 1937 it entered Yan'an without a fight, after local warlords handed the town over, and on 6 September 1937 Yan'an became the seat of the communist government of the Shaanxi-Gansu-Ningxia Border Region. Thousands of intellectuals travelled there, party members and troops were trained, and in 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> launched training programmes that began the Yan'an Rectification Movement.</p><p>Japanese bombing destroyed almost every building except a pagoda, and most inhabitants lived in yaodongs. From 1944 to 1947 the United States Army Observation Group, the Dixie Mission, was stationed there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> forces briefly captured the city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, after the Communist leadership learned of the attack and withdrew; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> retook it on 21 April 1948.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yan'an is regarded by Chinese communists as the birthplace of the revolution, and the Yan'an Revolution Memorial Hall draws visitors who come to learn the party's history. Yan'an University is based in the city. Oil has been extracted from the Changqing Oil Field, part of the Ordos basin, since the early 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yan'an” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The speech promoted a political purge similar to the <b>Yan'an</b> Rectification Movement in China and became known as the “Juche speech”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/juche">Juche · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its melody came from a peasant love song of the Loess Plateau, &quot;Bai Ma Diao&quot; (White Horse Tune), also called &quot;Zhima You&quot; (Sesame Oil), which was widely sung around <b>Yan'an</b> in the 1930s and whose words often changed with the singer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li><li><blockquote>As a reward, Xiang Yu made him King of Di, ruling the old Shang commandery in northern Shaanxi from Gaonu near modern <b>Yan'an</b>, one of three kingdoms meant to contain Liu Bang.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-di-206-bc">Kingdom of Di (206 BC)</a></li></ul></section>
